    "available_at": "2026-11-05T17:58:21.487988+00:00",
    "body": "Supply chain leaders who take electricity for granted may face an expensive surprise.\nWhat’s Related\nGartner said Thursday that access to energy is becoming a major constraint on supply chain growth, as rising electricity demand runs into aging grid infrastructure and slow connection timelines. The research firm predicts that by 2029, most manufacturers without advanced energy management will see costs 20% to 30% higher because of price and supply volatility, network congestion, and capacity constraints.\n“Many supply chains continue to operate under the assumption that energy is an always-available input,” said Marta Muñoz, senior director analyst in Gartner’s Supply Chain practice. “That assumption is becoming increasingly difficult to sustain. Energy availability is emerging as a factor that can influence where organizations operate, how they expand , and whether they can execute automation and electrification strategies as planned.”\nNew demand from AI data centers and electric fleets is hitting a grid that’s already short on transmission capacity. On top of that, getting a new grid connection can be a long process.\nMORE FROM GARTNER: Nearly Half of Supply Chains Cut Staff Because of AI | Few Supply Chains Will Let AI Make Planning Calls by 2030\nDealing with grid limits\nMost energy management still relies on procurement contracts and assumes the grid will stay as reliable as it has been. Gartner says those strategies do nothing to address physical limits like grid congestion or local capacity shortages. Those limits are starting to shape where companies build and how fast they can grow.\nWhere the risk shows up\nGartner points to several ways energy constraints can hit operations:\nGrid outages can halt manufacturing and distribution.\nLong interconnection timelines can delay facility openings and expansions.\nLocal congestion can drive up facility-level energy costs and squeeze margins.\nLimited grid capacity can slow fleet electrification and automation projects.\nSuppliers in energy-strapped regions may face higher costs or lower output, and that ripples down the chain.\nWhat to do now\nGartner advises CSCOs to build energy into network design and resilience planning instead of leaving it to procurement.\n“CSCOs should begin treating energy availability as a core consideration in network design and growth planning,” said Muñoz. “This means evaluating energy constraints during site selection, aligning energy strategies with local grid realities and incorporating energy availability into broader supply chain resilience efforts.”\nIn practice, a good first step is asking the local utility about available capacity and connection timelines before you commit to a site. It’s also worth checking which of your key suppliers work in regions where the grid is already strained.",
